  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$4.97 billion** of Chapter 07 goods in 2025, down **15.5%** from a record $5.89 billion in 2024. Fresh vegetables are most of it: lettuce ($595 million), peppers, broccoli and cauliflower, onions, tomatoes and fresh potatoes ($327 million). Dried pulses (heading 0713: dry beans, lentils, dried peas and chickpeas) are the other big group at **$973 million**, down 30.6%. Canada buys **59%** of the total, mostly fresh vegetables, and Mexico 12%, led by dry beans and potatoes.\n\nFor most of the trade the tariff is zero: Canada and Mexico charge nothing on qualifying U.S. vegetables under USMCA, the Dominican Republic and Costa Rica nothing on U.S. beans under CAFTA-DR, the EU nothing on pulses and, since July 1, 2026, nothing on U.S. onions, fresh sweet potatoes and several other vegetables. The costly markets are China, where U.S. pulses pay about 42–48% once its 2018–19 counter-tariffs and its extra 10% on all U.S. goods are added, and India, which put a 30% duty on yellow peas from every country in November 2025; U.S. sales to India fell from $80.6 million in 2024 to **$6.7 million** in 2025. For fresh vegetables, the buyer’s plant-health rules usually decide access more than the tariff: the EU, for example, does not admit U.S. potatoes at all.",
